      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, i'm seeing a CrashLoopBackOff on one of our microservices in GKE. i've checked the basic pods status but i'm not sure where to look next. here are the last few lines of the logs:\n\n[error] connection refused: failed to connect to database at 10.52.0.4:5432\n[error] retrying in 5s...\n[error] connection refused: failed to connect to database at 10.52.0.4:5432\n\ncan u give me a checklist of what to check to see if this is a resource issue, a liveness/readiness probe thing, or just a bad config?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "1. Check if the pods are on the 'CrashLoopBackOff' or 'Unknown' state: `kubectl get pods -namespace [your-namespace]`\n2. Look for out-of-memory (OOM) or low disk issues that might be preventing the pod from starting properly.\n3. Examine liveness and readiness probes for each container in the pod to see if they're failing. Use `kubectl describe pod [pod-name] -n [namespace]`\n4. Check environment variables, especially database connection strings and IP addresses, to ensure they're set with the right values for the current environment.\n5. If the pod is constantly crashing and remaining unable to recover, consider moving into a debug/rebuild workflow, such as restarting the pod, deploying a newer version of the container, or even deleting the existing pods and applying a new manifest with any necessary changes.\n6. Review the database connection attempts and configurations that might lead to a service being unreachable (DNS resolution issues, port blocking, incorrect service endpoints, etc.).",
      "source": "target",
