      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To differentiate between a resource limit being hit and the node being under pressure due to resource exhaustion, you can take the following actions and check these things:\n\n1. Check for Overcommit Warning: Run `kubectl describe node [node-name]` and look for “Warning: Out of Memory or Swap” messages. If it exists, it indicates that the node was heavily using CPU or memory resources and hit one of the limits.\n2. Review Memory Pressure: Similar items found in the \"Out of Memory or Swap\" warning indicate whether you're getting swapped,  allocation failed…other, or Out of Memory available…low.\n3. Look at Node Resource Stats: Use `kubectl top nodes --sort-by=cpu` to sort nodes by CPU usage. Add `--sort-by=memory` to sort by memory and `--containers` for an even more granular view.\n4. Examine Pod Resource Limits: Within the manifest (`deployment.yaml`) of your microservice pod, check for requests, limits, and other resource configurations (like `maxMemory`, `maxPods`, CPU shares, etc.).\n5. Check Node Logs: Look at the logs for the node to check for any failure messages or indicators of resource pressure (`/var/log/k",
